  ans: 1,
  exp: "The test described is the Tzanck smear, a rapid, bedside cytological technique. In Pemphigus Vulgaris, scraping the base of a flaccid blister reveals detached, rounded-up epidermal keratinocytes that have lost their intercellular connections. These are called Acantholytic cells or Tzanck cells. They characteristically display a large, hyperchromatic nucleus with a prominent nucleolus and condensed peripheral cytoplasm (perinuclear halo).",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Multinucleated giant cells):</strong> These are found on a Tzanck smear from lesions caused by Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV) or Varicella-Zoster Virus (VZV), not pemphigus.<br>• <strong>Option C (KOH preparation; Clue cells):</strong> KOH is used for fungal infections. Clue cells (epithelial cells studded with bacteria) are seen in wet mounts of vaginal discharge in Bacterial Vaginosis.<br>• <strong>Option D (Dark-field microscopy; Sézary cells):</strong> Dark-field microscopy is used to visualize Treponema pallidum (syphilis spirochetes). Sézary cells are atypical T-lymphocytes with cerebriform nuclei seen in blood smears of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."
