  ans: 1,
  exp: "The patient has Mycosis Fungoides (MF), the most common type of Cutaneous T-cell Lymphoma (CTCL). It classically progresses over years from an indolent 'patch' stage (often misdiagnosed as eczema or psoriasis) to a 'plaque' stage, and finally a 'tumor' stage. The hallmark histological finding is epidermotropism—the migration of atypical, neoplastic CD4+ T-cells (Sézary cells, possessing characteristic 'cerebriform' or brain-like folded nuclei) into the epidermis. When these atypical lymphocytes cluster together within the epidermis, they form pathognomonic Pautrier microabscesses.",
