  ans: 1,
  exp: "The patient has Alopecia Areata, a T-cell mediated autoimmune disease targeting the anagen hair follicles. It typically presents as rapid, asymptomatic, well-circumscribed patches of non-scarring hair loss. The pathognomonic clinical sign is the presence of 'exclamation mark hairs' at the active margins of the bald patches. These are short (3-4 mm), broken hairs that are thick at the distal pigmented end and taper to a thin, depigmented base (resembling an exclamation mark '!'), indicating active inflammatory damage to the hair shaft matrix.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Vellus hairs):</strong> Fine, light-colored, short hairs ('peach fuzz') that cover most of the body; in androgenetic alopecia, thick terminal hairs undergo miniaturization into vellus hairs.<br>• <strong>Option C (Corkscrew hairs):</strong> Coiled, twisted hairs pathognomonic for Scurvy (Vitamin C deficiency) due to defective collagen synthesis affecting the hair follicle structure.<br>• <strong>Option D (Bamboo hairs):</strong> Microscopic nodular swelling of the hair shaft (Trichorrhexis invaginata) characteristic of Netherton syndrome, causing severe hair fragility."
